Fix axe-core resolving relative @import URLs against the document base URL
==========================================================================

Target: axe-core v4.11.3 (vendored at BlazingStory.Addons.BuiltIns/wwwroot/js/)
Upstream status: not reported yet (as of 2026-07-29)

PROBLEM
-------
When axe.run() preloads the CSSOM, axe-core walks every stylesheet and fetches
the targets of its @import rules. It passes CSSImportRule.href straight to
XMLHttpRequest.open():

    lib/core/utils/parse-sameorigin-stylesheet.js
      .map(rule => rule.href)
      -> parseCrossOriginStylesheet(importUrl, ...)  ->  request.open('GET', url)

Per the CSSOM spec, CSSImportRule.href returns the URL exactly as written in the
stylesheet, which is usually relative. XMLHttpRequest resolves a relative URL
against the *document* base URL, whereas CSS resolves it against the URL of the
stylesheet that contains the @import. When those two differ, axe-core requests a
URL that does not exist and the browser console fills up with 404s.

Example seen in Samples/MyBlazorWasmApp1:

    /_content/MyBlazorWasmApp1/css/app.css contains  @import url("fonts/fonts.css");
    the browser correctly loads  /_content/MyBlazorWasmApp1/css/fonts/fonts.css
    axe-core requests           /fonts/fonts.css                        -> 404

The failure is swallowed by axe-core (it logs "Couldn't load preload assets" and
carries on), so this is console noise plus a loss of preloaded CSSOM data rather
than a broken accessibility check. It affects any consuming application whose
stylesheets use relative @import, not just the sample app.

FIX
---
Resolve the href against the containing stylesheet's own URL before handing it to
the network layer, falling back to the document base URL for @import rules inside
inline <style> elements (where sheet.href is null, and the document base URL is
in fact the correct base).

The `isCrossOriginRequest` regex test below the patched code is deliberately left
untouched: it still runs against the resolved URL, and the resulting flag is only
stored on the asset object and never read by any rule in 4.11.3.

APPLYING TO A NEWER axe-core
----------------------------
1. Drop the new upstream axe.js / axe.min.js / axe.d.ts into wwwroot/js/.
2. From the repository root, run:  git apply BlazingStory.Addons.BuiltIns/patches/axe-core-resolve-css-import-url.patch
   The diff below covers axe.js only.
3. axe.min.js has no usable line-based diff (the whole bundle is one line), so
   apply it by hand. The minified identifiers change on every upstream release,
   so re-locate the code rather than trusting these exact strings. In 4.11.3 the
   enclosing function is `cf=function(e,n,a,o){...Array.from(e.cssRules)...}`,
   where `e` is the stylesheet. Search for:

       .filter(function(e){return e.href}).map(function(e){return e.href})

   and replace it with:

       .filter(function(e){return e.href}).map(function(s){try{return new URL(s.href,e.href||document.baseURI).href}catch(u){return s.href}})

   The callback parameter must NOT be named `e`, because the replacement needs
   `e` to keep referring to the enclosing stylesheet argument.
4. Re-add the "NOTICE: This file has been modified" comment to the top of
   axe.min.js as well (step 2 handles axe.js).
5. Verify with `node --check` on both files, then load a story page and confirm
   no unexpected 404 for an @import target appears in the preview iframe.

